data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc`'d `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`.** Both work as expected:

```csharp
Span<nint> signedSpan = stackalloc nint[5];
Span<nuint> unsignedSpan = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

On this 64-bit system, each element is 8 bytes. The only caveat is that `sizeof(nint)` requires an `unsafe` context (since the size isn't known at compile time), but the stackalloc itself works in safe code just fine.
